linux-atm: silence the last Q_REMOVE lvalue warning

setNetPrefix() declares `NetPrefixNode *tmp = (NetPrefixNode *) var->value;`
and passes it to Q_REMOVE(), which uses it as the lvalue for the list-head
update. gcc 15 sees the value assigned but never read and reports
`-Wunused-but-set-variable` at atmf_uni.c:245.

Patch 706 deliberately kept the declaration because deleting it would
change the macro expansion. Add a bare `(void)tmp;` immediately after the
macro call instead: it is a real read of the variable, leaves the list
operation and its (unused) head-update result exactly as before, and is
the last warning in the linux-atm package.

Verified with a gcc 15.2 minimal repro that `(void)tmp;` clears
-Wunused-but-set-variable for a set-only local.

--- a/src/ilmid/atmf_uni.c
+++ b/src/ilmid/atmf_uni.c
@@ -245,6 +245,7 @@
 {
       NetPrefixNode *tmp = (NetPrefixNode *) var->value;
       Q_REMOVE(tmp, prefix);
+      (void)tmp;
 }
     }
